{"kind":"stack","id":"water-cycle-1","title":"The water cycle: evaporation and condensation","theme":{"background":"#e8f4fb","foreground":"#1b3a4b","font":"Arial, sans-serif"}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c1","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c1-t","content":"Evaporation"},{"type":"text","id":"c1-e","content":"Liquid water turns into water vapour when it is heated by the sun. The water goes into the air."},{"type":"button","id":"c1-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c2"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c2","kind":"flash","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c2-t","content":"Condensation"},{"type":"text","id":"c2-e","content":"Water vapour cools and turns back into tiny liquid droplets. These droplets form clouds."},{"type":"button","id":"c2-b","label":"Next Card","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c3"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c3","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c3-q","content":"What is the name of the process when liquid water turns into water vapour?"},{"type":"button","id":"c3-a","label":"Evaporation","why":"Evaporation is when heat turns liquid water into vapour.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-b","label":"Condensation","why":"Pupils mix it up with the opposite process, where vapour turns to liquid.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c3-c","label":"Precipitation","why":"Pupils mix it up with rain and snow falling from clouds.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c4"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c4","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c4-q","content":"What happens to water vapour when it cools in the sky?"},{"type":"button","id":"c4-a","label":"It turns back into tiny liquid droplets","why":"Cooling vapour condenses into droplets, which form clouds.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-b","label":"It turns into a solid block of ice","why":"Pupils wrongly think all cold water becomes solid ice.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c4-c","label":"It disappears completely","why":"Pupils forget that the water still exists, just in another form.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c5"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c5","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"image","picto":"sun"},{"type":"text","id":"c5-q","content":"What does the sun do in the water cycle?"},{"type":"button","id":"c5-a","label":"It heats water so it evaporates","why":"Heat from the sun gives water the energy to become vapour.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-b","label":"It makes water freeze","why":"Pupils confuse the sun with cold weather, which freezes water.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c5-c","label":"It makes clouds turn into rain","why":"Pupils think the sun causes rain directly, not cooling.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c6"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c6","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c6-q","content":"On a hot sunny day, does a puddle dry up faster or slower than on a cold day?"},{"type":"button","id":"c6-a","label":"Faster","why":"Higher temperature speeds up evaporation.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c6-b","label":"Slower","why":"Pupils think heat makes water stay put, not change state.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c7"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c7","kind":"quiz","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c7-q","content":"Which of these would make water evaporate the fastest?"},{"type":"button","id":"c7-a","label":"Warm water spread out in a wide dish","why":"Heat and a large surface both speed up evaporation.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":1},{"type":"sound","sound":"cheer"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-b","label":"Cold water in a small cup with a lid","why":"Pupils forget that cold and covered water evaporates slowly.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c7-c","label":"Cold water in a tall jar","why":"Pupils think depth matters more than surface area and temperature.","actions":[{"type":"score","delta":0},{"type":"sound","sound":"buzz"},{"type":"goto","cardId":"c8"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c8","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c8-t","content":"You are a water drop on a warm lake. A breeze blows. Do you rise up as vapour or sink to the bottom?"},{"type":"button","id":"c8-a","label":"Rise up as vapour","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c9"}]},{"type":"button","id":"c8-b","label":"Sink to the bottom","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c10"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c9","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c9-t","content":"You rise into the air, cool down and turn into a droplet in a cloud. Well done."},{"type":"button","id":"c9-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c10","kind":"story","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c10-t","content":"You stay in the lake. The sun is weak, so you wait a long time before you rise."},{"type":"button","id":"c10-b","label":"Continue","actions":[{"type":"goto","cardId":"c11"}]}],"actions":[]}}
{"kind":"card","card":{"id":"c11","kind":"end","elements":[{"type":"text","id":"c11-h","content":"You finished the water cycle lesson"},{"type":"text","id":"c11-m","content":"Great work! You now know how evaporation and condensation move water around the Earth."}],"actions":[]}}
